"explanation": "The statement places \\(A,B,O\\) on one line and \\(C,D,O\\) on another, but does not specify the point order. Thus \\(OA,OB\\) can be the same ray or opposite rays, and likewise for \\(OC,OD\\).\n\nIf both pairs are opposite rays, the named angles are vertical and equal. If both pairs are the same ray, the named angles coincide and are again equal. In either case,\n\\[\n4x-15=2x+35,\n\\]\nso \\(x=25\\) and \\(\\angle AOC=4\\times25-15=85^\\circ\\).\n\nIf exactly one pair is opposite, the named angles are supplementary. Therefore\n\\[\n(4x-15)+(2x+35)=180,\n\\]\nwhich gives \\(6x=160\\), \\(x=80/3\\), and\n\\[\n\\angle AOC=\\left(\\frac{275}{3}\\right)^\\circ.\n\\]\nThis case is possible with \\(A,B\\) on opposite sides of \\(O\\) and \\(C,D\\) on the same ray; the other angle is \\((265/3)^\\circ\\), so both angles are valid.\n\nThese cases exhaust the same-ray and opposite-ray possibilities. Hence the displayed question has no unique answer: \\(85^\\circ\\) follows under the intended opposite-ray arrangement, while another permitted arrangement gives \\((275/3)^\\circ\\).",
