"gradingStatus": "disputed",
"gradingNote": "This question does not identify one unambiguous answer. You can study it without grading.",
"solution": "Work in the real numbers and distinguish all square roots from the principal square root. A positive number has two square roots (the square roots of 4 are \\(\\pm2\\)), the principal square root takes only the positive value 2, and negative numbers have no real square roots. Thus B and C are false. The only square root of 0 is 0, so D is true.\n\nHowever, A's phrase “the square root of 4” can mean the principal square root in ordinary English. Under that reading, \\(\\sqrt{4}=2\\), so A and D are both correct. If A is intended to give all real square roots of 4, it omits \\(-2\\), and D alone is correct. The stored key is D, but the displayed English does not support a unique single-choice answer.",
