"explanation": "ASA requires two equal angle pairs and equal included sides. The endpoints of \\(AB\\) are \\(A,B\\), and those of \\(DE\\) are \\(D,E\\). Since \\(\\angle A=\\angle D\\) is given, choice D, \\(\\angle B=\\angle E\\), directly supplies the remaining angle equality and proves congruence by ASA.\n\nIf choice A is added instead, then \\(\\angle C=\\angle F\\). Together with the given \\(\\angle A=\\angle D\\), the triangle angle sum gives\n\\[\n\\angle B=180^\\circ-\\angle A-\\angle C\n=180^\\circ-\\angle D-\\angle F=\\angle E.\n\\]\nNow \\(\\angle A=\\angle D\\), \\(AB=DE\\), and \\(\\angle B=\\angle E\\) prove congruence by ASA. Thus both A and D allow an ASA proof. D is the intended direct completion, but the displayed question does not prohibit the intermediate angle-sum step.",
