"rejectedAnswers": [],
"unorderedAnswers": false,
"gradingStatus": "disputed",
"gradingNote": "This question does not identify one unambiguous answer. You can study it without grading.",
"solution": "Two figures that are reflections of each other coincide completely when folded along the axis of reflection, so they must be congruent. Thus D is true. Congruent figures are not necessarily related by a single reflection, and a reflected pair can lie entirely on opposite sides of the axis with no common point.\n\nIn C, if 'these two figures' refers to the two coinciding halves, the fold line reflects one half onto the other, so C is also true. If it refers to two whole figures, only one whole figure has been introduced. Consequently, the displayed wording does not justify D as the unique correct choice.",
