"id": "factor-and-reduce-with-domain",
"label": "Factor and cancel on the original domain",
"explanation": "The denominator requires \\(x\\ne-3\\). Factoring the numerator exposes the same factor as the denominator, so on this domain,\n\\[\n\\frac{x^2-9}{x+3}=\\frac{(x-3)(x+3)}{x+3}=x-3.\n\\]\nThe fraction is therefore 0 exactly when \\(x-3=0\\), giving \\(x=3\\), which satisfies the denominator restriction.",
