"label": "Show that the two sides never agree",
"explanation": "Since \\(x^2-1=(x-1)(x+1)\\), the original equation is defined only for \\(x\\ne1,-1\\). Subtracting the right side from the left lets us test whether they can be equal. For every allowed \\(x\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{x-1}-\\frac{2}{x^2-1}\n=\\frac{x+1-2}{(x-1)(x+1)}\n=\\frac{x-1}{(x-1)(x+1)}\n=\\frac{1}{x+1}.\n\\]\nThe cancellation is valid because \\(x\\ne1\\). The final fraction has numerator \\(1\\) and a nonzero denominator, so it cannot equal zero. Thus the two sides are never equal, and the original equation has no solution, choice C.",
