"rejectedAnswers": [],
"unorderedAnswers": false,
"solution": "Assume the polygon is convex, with \\(n\\ge3\\) sides. From one vertex, exclude that vertex and its two neighbors, leaving \\(n-3\\) vertices to which diagonals can be drawn. These diagonals lie inside the polygon and meet only at the chosen vertex. Each diagonal adds one region, and after all are drawn each region is a triangle. Thus the number of triangles is\n\\[\n1+(n-3)=n-2.\n\\]\n\nThe question does not state the needed condition. For a counterexample, take a triangle \\(ABD\\) and a point \\(C\\) strictly inside it. Joining \\(A,B,C,D,A\\) forms a concave quadrilateral. From \\(B\\), the only diagonal is \\(BD\\), which lies outside the quadrilateral except at its endpoints, so it does not divide the interior into two triangles.\n\nTherefore, \\(n-2\\), choice B, holds for a convex polygon or whenever the chosen diagonals form an internal fan triangulation. It is not guaranteed by the statement as written.",
