"gradingStatus": "disputed",
"gradingNote": "This question does not identify one unambiguous answer. You can study it without grading.",
"solution": "If yellow is compared with each color separately, equal chances for the individual balls mean we can compare counts. The number of yellow balls must exceed both the 6 red balls and the 4 white balls, so \\(x>6\\). Thus the smallest integer \\(x\\) is \\(7\\).\n\nIf “either a red ball or a white ball” means the two colors combined, there are \\(6+4=10\\) such balls. Yellow is more likely only when \\(x>10\\), so the smallest integer \\(x\\) is \\(11\\).\n\nTherefore the answer is \\(7\\) for separate comparisons and \\(11\\) for the combined-color comparison. The wording does not determine a unique answer.",
