"explanation": "If the labels \\(1\\) and \\(2\\) each correspond to exactly one elementary outcome, the two events each contain one outcome. They are mutually exclusive because one displayed number cannot be both \\(1\\) and \\(2\\), so their union contains \\(1+1=2\\) outcomes. Under this assumption, its two outcomes do not fill the six-outcome sample space, so the union is not certain.\n\nHowever, the labels are not specified. For example, drawing one of six distinct, equally likely cards labeled \\(1,1,2,3,4,5\\) gives two outcomes showing \\(1\\) and one showing \\(2\\). The two cards labeled \\(1\\) are distinct elementary outcomes. Their union therefore contains \\(2+1=3\\) outcomes, with no overlap. Thus the count is not determined as written; the additional assumption that each requested label occurs exactly once is sufficient to justify \\(2\\).",
