"label": "Compare the intersection with the product",
"explanation": "If both events have positive probability, mutual exclusivity gives\n\\[\nP(A\\cap B)=0<P(A)P(B).\n\\]\nThe defining equality for independence therefore fails, so under this assumption the answer is D.\n\nHowever, if “non-impossible” means only that the events are nonempty, positive probability is not guaranteed. Consider three distinct outcomes \\(a,b,c\\) with probabilities \\(0,\\frac12,\\frac12\\), respectively. Let \\(A=\\{a\\}\\) and \\(B=\\{b\\}\\). These events are nonempty and disjoint, but\n\\[\nP(A\\cap B)=0=0\\cdot\\frac12=P(A)P(B),\n\\]\nso they are independent. Thus no choice follows universally from the statement alone. Choice D requires both event probabilities to be positive, as they would be for nonempty events in a finite equally likely model.",
