"solution": "Under the recorded convention that a linear function includes every affine function \\(y=mx+b\\), including constants, let \\(a_1\\) be the first term and \\(d\\) the common difference. Then\n\\[\na_n=a_1+(n-1)d=dn+(a_1-d).\n\\]\nThe general term is a linear expression in \\(n\\), so these points lie on a straight line. Specifically, the sequence graph consists of the isolated points \\((n,a_n)\\), for positive integers \\(n\\), on \\(y=dx+(a_1-d)\\). Under this convention, the answer is A.\n\nIf a linear function is required to have nonzero slope, this conclusion needs \\(d\\ne0\\). When \\(d=0\\), for example \\(a_n=1\\), the graph lies on the constant-function line \\(y=1\\), which that definition excludes. The question permits this case, so A remains convention-dependent.",
